# Azure App Service Deployment Plan

## Goal
Prepare the existing Spring Boot WAR application for Azure App Service deployment, validate the deployment package, and document the remaining manual steps.

## Project Assessment
- Application: Spring Boot web app
- Packaging: WAR
- Java runtime: 21
- Deployment target: Azure App Service (Linux)
- Existing deployment assets:
  - [azure.yaml](../azure.yaml)
  - [infra/appservice.bicep](../infra/appservice.bicep)
  - [.github/workflows/azure-app-service.yml](../.github/workflows/azure-app-service.yml)
  - [src/main/resources/application.properties](../src/main/resources/application.properties)

## Validation Completed
- Maven package build was executed successfully and produced a deployable WAR artifact at [target/banking-demo-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.war](../target/banking-demo-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.war).
- Application source files were checked for editor-reported issues and no Java errors were found.

## Deployment Steps
1. Create or select an Azure resource group and region.
2. Deploy the App Service infrastructure with the existing Bicep template:
   - `az group create --name <rg-name> --location <region>`
   - `az deployment group create --resource-group <rg-name> --template-file infra/appservice.bicep --parameters webAppName=<app-name>`
3. Configure GitHub Actions secrets for deployment:
   - `AZURE_CLIENT_ID`
   - `AZURE_TENANT_ID`
   - `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID`
   - `AZURE_WEBAPP_NAME`
4. Run the workflow or deploy the WAR artifact manually with Azure CLI or the App Service deploy action.
5. Validate the app at `https://<app-name>.azurewebsites.net/health`.

## Remaining Manual Actions
- Provision the Azure App Service resources if they do not already exist.
- Supply the Azure credentials and App Service name to the workflow secrets.
- Trigger the workflow or perform a manual deployment from the built WAR package.
